3. I did the analysis to get a rough estimate of the post-pit melt. 
Essentially I took the annual balance, converted it to snow depth, and subtracted it from the pit depth. 
So these results rely on the USGS melt factors/lapse rates. 
If you want to dig into that information further, a good start would be looking at `JIRP_MASSBALANCE_2024/JIRP-Mass-Balance/glacier-mass-balance-code/data/Taku/Output/Output_Taku_Adjusted_Point_Balances`.
It's hard to say whether this will be a higher or lower melt year, but you might be able to get a sense of it with the pit depths so far.
The NOAA seasonal forecast forecasts even chances for higher/lower temperatures than average.
It forecasts a slightly higher chance of above average precipitation.
To summarize the results: above 1500 m, the median post-pit melt is under 1 m.
Sites below 1200 m have a median post-pit melt of 2 or more meters.
Looking at specific sites, the ones which seem to have the highest risk of melting out are the ones in front of C10.
One solution to this is putting stakes on Taku A and B. 
Another interesting solution would be to drill the stake hole at the bottom of the mass balance pits.
The resulting bias might be worth it, but I don't have a sense for how long it would be until the data are reliable. 
My guess is that wouldn't happen until after the end of the melt season.
Filling in the pit would help, and might result in reliable data in a month or so?
If you go that route, I'd suggest also using some of the old pendants to put a stake on Taku A and/or B so that there is some reliable data from that area regardless.
I put a report containing plots in this folder that will hopefully be helpful for making decisions about specific sites.


4. As you suggested, I made a map of proposed stake locations and assigned them a priority level.
This was really helpful!
More pieces are fitting together mentally, and I'm developing a better sense of what combinations of locations might be more useful for analysis. 
Most of the locations I'm suggesting coincide with mass balance pits, though there are a few additions. 
Points are either: i) on the primary transect along the traverse path, ii) stradling a significant topographic feature, or iii) to investigate whether points along the primary transect are representative. 
Locations in the last category vary one "facet" (e.g., elevation, margin vs. centerline, glacier branch/basin) of the primary stake but are otherwise meant to be approximately equivalent. 
Additionally, there are many pairs of stakes which we can reasonably expect to have similar amounts of cloud cover.
For these, the new pendant on top of poles can be substituted for an old pendant (probably shielded?) to ensure there are enough of the new pendants for their improved light sensors to be distributed strategically. 
My thought is to save the new light sensors for camps (to compliment the microstations) and stakes on the primary transect where we expect qualitative changes in cloud cover. 
Examples I'm envisioning are scenarios like Lemon Creek vs. SW Branch Taku, or either side of the ice divide. 
The points and priorities are available as a KMZ that can be loaded into CalTopo as well as a spreadsheet. I've put an image of the points overlaid on a map into the lapse rate manual. 
Lastly, I want to emphasize again that these locations/priorities are very much a first pass, and you should make modifications as you and the students see fit!
